On 22 feb, 14:24, "Robert Jarratt" <nos...@nosp.am> wrote:
> > If the II has a TK50 you can make your own TK50 distribution, make
> > sure you enable tape serving on the microVAX II
> > when it is a clustermember.
> > Hans
>
> I have the facility to write to TK50 from other VAXen I have, but I am not
> sure what I need to do to make the tape bootable. Is it just a matter of
> writing the saveset to the tape?
>
> Also not clear about the tape serving comment, if I can make a TK50 tape and
> install VMS from that, why would I need to enable tape serving etc?
>
> Thanks
>
> Rob

installing from tape requires two things:
1 a standalone backup tape (SAB)
2 a tape with the VMSxxx.A etc savesets
The SAB tape is built by a VMS utility: sys$update:STABACKIT.COM
Run it and it asks the name of the target device, in your case the
TK50 drive (MUA0:, or MKA400 depending on yout platform)
Next copy the VMSnnn.% savesets to another tape.
Boot from the SAB tape. Next:
$ backup mua0:vmsnnn.B dua0:
after the backup is finished (a SAB backup command is by default /
image) reboot from dua0: (or whatever the name of the disk is);
rewind the VMS tape in the TK50 and continue the installation
A little brief, if I'm too short on details, let me know.
Hans


