Rob,
it can be done! I ran VMS 4.7 including DECwindows (VWS) on an RD54 in
a VAXstation 2000, including DECnet, four
compilers and Rdb and still had enough diskspace to write software.
Another VS 2000 runs VMS 5.5-1
on an RD53.
An RD53 is a tight squeeze for VMS V7.3 but it might just work. A
microVAX II is a hefty piece piece
of equipment but you're not going to use it for real work, right?
If that's your intention then an RD53 is enough to get VMS up.
During the installation process just answer NO to all the questions
that add components. Leave off the
on line documentation, examples, and keep the help libraries
compressed. VMS comes with LAT built in
so networking is possible. DECnet phase IV is fairly small, you might
be able to have that too.
If there is another RD53, use it to store the swapfile and pagefile.
Use VMSTAILOR to reduce components that you won't be using anyway,
it's a demonstration model.
The other question was about installing VMS. IIRC the microVAX II can
boot over ethernet.
You have other VMS systems, so configure one of these as a cluster
member and boot host.
Backup/image VMS073.B to the intended system disk, copy the other
savesets to its root.
If the II has a TK50 you can make your own TK50 distribution, make
sure you enable tape serving on the microVAX II
when it is a clustermember.
Hans


