Good luck with the power supply.

MicroVAX IIs can iirc do a network boot - I'm pretty sure they were
viable cluster satellites in the early days of NI clusters (or LAVCs
or whatever you wish to call them), and I'm pretty sure they also
could be used to network boot a VAXeln system.

If you have the choice of DELQA or DEQNA, DELQA wins. There were
"issues" with DEQNA locking up in certain circumstances. I don't
remember the exact details, but things were bad enough that where I
worked, all DEQNAs were eventually replaced with DELQAs. You'll find
some details by searching newsgroups for DEQNA lockup.

The KZQSA was a Qbus adapter for SCSI but was very limited in
connectivity (intended for CD reader and tape?). A 3rd party SCSI card
might be more practical.

Earlier,  Philip said "In older versions of VMS, you could get by with
120 MB or so IF you didn't install everything and IF you installed it
on a larger disk first and then used VMSTAILOR to cut it down."

The principle is fine, the numbers not so fine. I learnt VMS on a VAX
725 on VMS V3.7. The VAX 725 system disk was one of the two drives in
an RZ25, each drive (one fixed, one removable) having its own 26MB.
You could still get enough VMS on the fixed 26MB drive to do software
development and be supported, probably even when VMS V4 came out. You
wouldn't chose this config if you had other options, but at that time
it was the cheapest VMS box.


